@use-modules[(forest-category commands)]

@title{Remarks on the Definition}
@summary{Notes adapted from cat-0002.tree, linked to the category definition.}
@published{2024-05-21}

@paragraph{These remarks accompany @link["cat-0001"]{the definition of a category}.}

@heading{Smallness depends on a model}
@paragraph{The definition is independent of any particular model of set theory. Once a model is chosen, the category is called small when both its objects and its morphisms form sets.}

@heading{Composition notation}
@paragraph{Some authors write @tex-math{fg} for @tex-math{f} followed by @tex-math{g}; here @tex-math{fg} means @tex-math{f} after @tex-math{g}. The domain and codomain rule in @link["cat-0001"]{the definition} fixes this convention.}

@heading{The laws in context}
@paragraph{Here are the identity and associativity laws used by the definition, embedded from the source tree:}
@transclude["unstable-0"]

@heading{An equivalent identity test}
@paragraph{A morphism @tex-math{f} is an identity exactly when composing it on the right leaves every compatible morphism unchanged and composing it on the left does the same. This lets us characterize identities using composition alone.}

@paragraph{The full axioms remain available on @link["cat-0001"]{the category page}.}
